From 2a50c5f0a875c3f870848f8ac2ababbb5d4ae3c2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Lajosh=C3=A1zi=2C=20L=C3=A1szl=C3=B3=20Gergely?=
 <lajoshazilg@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 21:15:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] camera: take the picture at the sensor's resolution, not
 the preview's

The source's capsfilter is shared by the viewfinder and by still capture, so
the two have always had one resolution between them: whatever is smooth enough
to preview is also what gets saved. On a phone with a 12 MP sensor and a
software ISP the two numbers are nowhere near each other - measured on a
Fairphone 3, the pipeline sustains about 6 fps at the sensor's full resolution
and about 30 fps at 1920x1080, which is what it was quietly saving pictures at.

Do what Megapixels does: define a preview mode and a capture mode, and switch
between them for the shot. Its documentation states the trade-off exactly -
"usually the maximum resolution of the sensor will be used for taking pictures
but at that resolution the framerate will generally be too low for realtime
preview" - and it picks "the resolution closest to the display resolution as
the preview mode".
<https://libmegapixels-12f3eb.pages.debian.net/config.html>

The switch has to happen on the source. Setting camerabin's image-capture-caps
instead asks the running pipeline to renegotiate, and a PipeWire source cannot:
it answers 'streaming stopped, reason not-negotiated (-4)', the viewfinder
freezes and no picture is saved. So take_picture() constrains the source to the
largest resolution, waits for the pipeline to come back up, fires the capture,
and image-done puts the preview resolution back. If the pipeline does not come
back up in five seconds the shot is taken anyway, because a picture at the
wrong resolution beats a shutter press that does nothing.

The preview resolution is chosen once, from what the camera offers, and left
alone. Adapting it to the frame rate was tried and removed: dropping to the
next size the camera advertises walks into sizes this pipeline cannot actually
stream, and the failure is not graceful. Measured on the Fairphone 3, where a
dark room is enough to make the preview slow: the source was reconfigured from
1920x1080 to 1600x1200 to 1680x1050 within seconds and then refused to start
with 'Failed to start streaming: Resource busy', leaving the application with
no camera at all. A slow viewfinder is a much better outcome than that, and a
size ladder that a pipeline handler advertises is evidently not a promise that
every rung can be streamed.
